WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.140
<v Speaker>So now let's learn</v>

2
00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.300
about encryption in Amazon S3.

3
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.340
So you have different methods to do so,

4
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.590
and we're going to learn about them in this lecture.

5
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.320
So you have server-side encryption SSC

6
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.240
and you have different flavors of it.

7
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.180
You have SSE-S3, which is enabled by default,

8
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.355
which is server-side encryption with Amazon S3 manage keys.

9
00:00:18.355 --> 00:00:22.170
You have SSE-KMS where you use KMS keys

10
00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.874
stored in AWS-KMS to do the encryption.

11
00:00:24.874 --> 00:00:28.494
You have DSSE-KMS, which is a dual layer

12
00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:30.210
server-side encryption.

13
00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.650
So we'll learn about this as well.

14
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.950
We have SSE-C where the server side-encryption is done

15
00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.583
with the customer provided keys

16
00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:38.513
and then you have client-side encryption.

17
00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:40.500
So you're supposed to understand which one is

18
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.240
for which at the exam,

19
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.770
but we're going to do a deep dive

20
00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.360
into those in this lecture.

21
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.730
So don't worry right now if you don't understand

22
00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.050
any of what I'm saying.

23
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.336
So let's go ahead and learn about the next one.

24
00:00:51.336 --> 00:00:55.767
So the first one is Amazon S3 for SSE-S3 encryption.

25
00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:59.490
So in this case, the encryption is using a key

26
00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:03.030
that's handled, managed, and owned by AWS.

27
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.920
You never have access to this key.

28
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.534
The object is going to be encrypted server-side by AWS

29
00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:13.080
and the security type of the encryption is AES-256.

30
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.000
Therefore, you must set the header

31
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.000
to X-amz-server-side-encryption AES256 to request Amazon S3

32
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.650
to encrypt the object for you using the SSE-S3 mechanism.

33
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.110
Now SSE-S3 is enabled by default

34
00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.572
for new buckets and new objects.

35
00:01:30.572 --> 00:01:31.950
So how does that work?

36
00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.003
We have Amazon S3 and with our user,

37
00:01:34.003 --> 00:01:36.390
the user you, you're going

38
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.120
to upload the file with the correct header

39
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.000
and then it will be an object under Amazon S3.

40
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.020
Amazon S3 will pair it with the S3 owned key, okay?

41
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.600
Because we're using the SSE-S3 mechanism

42
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.510
and then we'll perform encryption by mixing the key

43
00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.070
and the object and that will be

44
00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.590
what will be stored on your S3 buckets.

45
00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.290
So that's for the simpler one SSE-S3.

46
00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.940
Then we have SSE-KMS.

47
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.352
So this time instead of relying on the key that is owned

48
00:02:02.352 --> 00:02:05.430
by AWS and the S3 service, you want

49
00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:09.094
to manage your own keys yourself using the KMS service,

50
00:02:09.094 --> 00:02:11.460
the key management service.

51
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.320
So the advantages of using KMS is

52
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.240
that you have user control over this key

53
00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.120
so you can create keys yourself within KMS

54
00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.370
and you can audit the key usage using CloudTrail.

55
00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.000
So anytime someone uses a key in KMS, this is going

56
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.460
to be logged in a service that logs everything

57
00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.010
that happens in AWS called CloudTrail.

58
00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.772
So for this, we must have a header

59
00:02:31.772 --> 00:02:36.660
called the X-amz-server-side-encryption AWS KMS

60
00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.300
and then the object will be encrypted server-side.

61
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.030
So anything SSE, of course, is server-side.

62
00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.110
So how does that work?

63
00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.300
Again, we upload the object this time

64
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.310
with a different header and in the header,

65
00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.310
we actually specify the KMS key we want to use.

66
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.910
Then the object is appearing in Amazon S3.

67
00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.800
And this time the KMS key that's going

68
00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.060
to be used is coming out of the AWS KMS.

69
00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.210
So these two things together are going to be blended

70
00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.980
and then you're going to get encryption

71
00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.060
and that's the file that's going

72
00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.040
to end up in the S3 buckets.

73
00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.982
So now to read that file from the S3 bucket,

74
00:03:09.982 --> 00:03:12.922
not only do you need access to the object itself,

75
00:03:12.922 --> 00:03:16.132
but also to the underlying KMS key that was used

76
00:03:16.132 --> 00:03:17.760
to encrypt this object.

77
00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.559
So this is another level of security.

78
00:03:20.559 --> 00:03:23.081
So SSE-KMS has some limitations

79
00:03:23.081 --> 00:03:25.770
because well, now that you upload

80
00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:28.710
and download files from Amazon S3, you need

81
00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.052
to leverage a KMS key.

82
00:03:30.052 --> 00:03:32.880
And the KMS key has its own APIs,

83
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.227
for example, generate data key.

84
00:03:34.227 --> 00:03:37.091
And when you decrypt, you're going to use the decrypt API.

85
00:03:37.091 --> 00:03:38.940
And so therefore, you're going

86
00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.743
to do API calls into the KMS service.

87
00:03:41.743 --> 00:03:44.280
And each of these API calls is going

88
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.972
to count towards the KMS quotas of API calls per second.

89
00:03:47.972 --> 00:03:50.773
So based on the region, you have between 5,000

90
00:03:50.773 --> 00:03:52.800
and 30,000 requests per second,

91
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.089
although they can be increased

92
00:03:54.089 --> 00:03:56.210
using the service quotas console.

93
00:03:56.210 --> 00:04:00.569
And so if you have a very, very high-throughput S3 bucket

94
00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:03.131
and everything is encrypted using KMS keys,

95
00:04:03.131 --> 00:04:06.420
you may go into a threat link kind of use case.

96
00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.180
So this is something the exam may test you on.

97
00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.600
So next, we have DSSE-KMS.

98
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.920
The idea is that it's DSSE

99
00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.094
for dual-layer-server-side-encryption.

100
00:04:16.094 --> 00:04:19.170
And the idea is that you're going to encrypt the four

101
00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:20.670
because there's no word dual.

102
00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.040
We're going to encrypt the object twice.

103
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.641
First with thanks to the KMS key service

104
00:04:25.641 --> 00:04:27.960
and then S3 manage keys.

105
00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.120
So the object is going to go through first level

106
00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.370
of encryption, a second level of encryption,

107
00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.893
and then it's going to be sent to the S3 bucket.

108
00:04:34.893 --> 00:04:38.054
So the reason why we have this is sometimes at exam,

109
00:04:38.054 --> 00:04:41.400
you will be required to have multilayer encryption

110
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.730
and then this is going to be the right answer, DSSE-KMS.

111
00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:46.620
For this, you just send the object

112
00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.510
with the header like this,

113
00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:51.932
AWS KMS-DSSE, and you're good to go.

114
00:04:51.932 --> 00:04:55.996
Next we have the SSE-C-type of encryption.

115
00:04:55.996 --> 00:05:00.540
So this time the keys are managed outside of AWS,

116
00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.215
but it's still server-side encryption

117
00:05:02.215 --> 00:05:05.610
because we send the key to AWS.

118
00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.380
But Amazon S3 will never store

119
00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.850
the encryption key you provide.

120
00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.220
After they're used, they're being discarded.

121
00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:14.820
So in that case, because we transmit a key into Amazon S3,

122
00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.283
we must use HTTPS

123
00:05:16.283 --> 00:05:19.560
and we must pass the key as part of HTTP headers

124
00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.810
for every request being made.

125
00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:22.830
So how does that work?

126
00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:26.850
The user is going to upload a file as well as the key,

127
00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:29.163
but the user manages the key outside of AWS.

128
00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:33.450
And then Amazon S3 will use the client's provided key

129
00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:36.180
and the object to perform some encryption

130
00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.480
and then put the file as encrypted into an S3 bucket.

131
00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.220
And, of course, to read that file,

132
00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.140
the user must again provide the key

133
00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:44.862
that was used to encrypt that file.

134
00:05:44.862 --> 00:05:48.795
Finally, we have the client-side encryption.

135
00:05:48.795 --> 00:05:51.510
So this is easier to implement if we leverage

136
00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.410
some client library

137
00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.260
such as the client-side encryption library.

138
00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.090
And the idea with client-side encryption is

139
00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.300
that the clients must encrypt data themselves

140
00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.868
before sending data to Amazon S3.

141
00:06:02.868 --> 00:06:05.870
And also you can retrieve the data from Amazon S3

142
00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.210
and then the decryption of the data happens on the client

143
00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.400
outside of Amazon S3.

144
00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.539
Therefore, the client fully manages the keys

145
00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:15.591
and the encryption cycle.

146
00:06:15.591 --> 00:06:16.950
So how does that work?

147
00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:18.030
We have a file

148
00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:21.360
and we have a client key that's outside of AWS.

149
00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.199
The client itself is going to provide

150
00:06:23.199 --> 00:06:25.230
and perform the encryption.

151
00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.120
So now we have an encrypted file

152
00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.466
and that file as is can be sent into Amazon S3 for upload.

153
00:06:31.466 --> 00:06:34.830
So we've seen all the levels of encryption of objects,

154
00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:38.040
but now let's talk about encryption in transit.

155
00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.360
So encryption in transit

156
00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.541
or in flight is also called SSL or TLS.

157
00:06:42.541 --> 00:06:46.290
And basically, your Amazon S3 bucket has two endpoints,

158
00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.918
the HTTP endpoint that is not encrypted

159
00:06:48.918 --> 00:06:53.010
and the HTTPS endpoint that has encryption in flights.

160
00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.410
So anytime you visit a website and you see that green lock

161
00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:58.110
or lock, usually that means it's using encryption

162
00:06:58.110 --> 00:06:59.927
in flights, meaning the connection between you

163
00:06:59.927 --> 00:07:03.870
and the target server is secure and fully-encrypted.

164
00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.514
And therefore, when you're using Amazon S3,

165
00:07:05.514 --> 00:07:08.460
it's fully recommended to use HTTPS

166
00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.706
to have secure transmission of data, of course.

167
00:07:10.706 --> 00:07:14.640
And if you use the SSE-C-type of mechanism,

168
00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:18.030
you must use the HTTPS protocol.

169
00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.160
Now, this is not something to worry about in real-life

170
00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.767
because well, most clients would use the HTTPS endpoint

171
00:07:23.767 --> 00:07:25.027
by default.

172
00:07:25.027 --> 00:07:29.066
Now, how would you go about forcing encryption in transit?

173
00:07:29.066 --> 00:07:31.776
For this, we could use a bucket policy.

174
00:07:31.776 --> 00:07:34.566
So you attach a bucket policy to your S3 bucket

175
00:07:34.566 --> 00:07:37.290
and you attach this statement, which is saying

176
00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:40.216
that you deny any get object operation

177
00:07:40.216 --> 00:07:45.216
if the condition is AWS secure transport false.

178
00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.245
So secure transport is going to be true

179
00:07:47.245 --> 00:07:49.320
whenever you're using HTTPS and false

180
00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.775
whenever you're not using an encrypted connection.

181
00:07:51.775 --> 00:07:54.960
And so therefore, any user trying to use HTTP

182
00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.550
on your bucket is going to be blocked,

183
00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:59.970
but users using HTTPS may be allowed.

184
00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:02.280
Okay, so that's it for encryption.

185
00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:05.553
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

